        Revision history:

        - 26th November 1996 -
        First release of Floods (v1.0)

        Notes: Remember this date friends... it's the beginning of a new way
               to flood!


        - 14th December 1996 -
        Second release of Floods (v1.1)
        -> Used a different method of displaying the log which is a lot faster
           and more reliable.  Code thanks to Zarquon.
        -> Added the Floods Advisor which will give you some advice on what
           type of floods to use, after asking some questions.
        -> Ported Floods to VB4.0.
        -> Change the looks of part of Floods, such as fonts, etc.
        -> Now you can resize the main menu.
        -> Found a way to optimise the flooding even more.
        -> Added a half second delay after sending messages and if sending of
           messages fails, there is a 10 time retry.  This is to prevent data
           loss due to lagged connections.
        -> In v1.0, all 3 Hurrican floods are reported as "(Light)", even
           though they are different.  Fixed.
        -> The Hurrican floods are improved.
        -> Added Floodnet / Botnet support.

        Notes: Floods is better than ever with its new Floodnet / Botnet
               ability!  Remember, if you want to be a registered use, send
               me a reason why you like Floods and an enhancement / bugfix you
               would like to see in Floods.  To be a supporter, you need to
               send 2 reasons and 2 enhancements / bugfixes.  Refer to Contact
               Information for more information.

        
        - 30th December 1996 -
        Maintainance release of Floods (v1.1.1)
        -> Added the date to release v1.1 in this file.
        -> Added error checking to prevent someone from connecting to himself.
        -> Previously, if a compound flood (A flood with more than one
           command, such as a CTCP KILL) is requested, it takes a long time
           before flooding actually occurs.  This is now fixed.
        -> Changed the way compound floods are carried out.  Previously
           compound flooding is carried out as below (For a 3 command flood
           with 3 bots):

           Bot one performs command one.
           Bot one performs command two.
           Bot one performs command three.
           Bot two performs command one.
           Bot two performs command two.
           Bot two performs command three.
           Bot three performs command one.
           Bot three performs command two.
           Bot three performs command three.

           Now, compound flooding is carried out as below:

           Bot one performs command one.
           (.1 second pause)
           Bot two performs command one.
           Bot one performs command two.
           (.1 second pause)
           Bot three performs command one.
           Bot two performs command two.
           Bot one performs command three.
           (.1 second pause)
           Bot three performs command two.
           Bot two performs command three.
           (.1 second pause)
           Bot three performs command three.

           This way, the chances of the bots getting killed is less.
        -> If you looked closely, sometimes the font in the log will change.
           Since the default font is a DOS font, (E.G: the character "W" has
           the same width as the character "I"), the changing of fonts may
           result in the log looking silly, especially if someone is sending
           you an ASCII art (IF he manages to bypass the flood protection that
           is).  This is now fixed.
        -> Fixed some bugs.

        Notes: Go to my page and check out my newest program, Avalanche!


        - 23rd January 1997 -
        Third release of Floods (v2.0)
        -> Added a new flood, Random, which will select a random flood from
           the list of floods.
        -> Changed some of the code, although the result is the same (a bit
           faster and uses less memory though).
        -> Fixed some bugs with the log.
        -> Revised floods.doc
        -> This may be the LAST version of Floods as I cannot seem to find
           anything to add to it.
